  {
   "id": "OvlLm6YPBwAJ",
   "from": "Ben Goodrich",
   "author": "743bbacc59f4b25f",
   "date": "2015-08-23T19:39:08Z",
   "subject": "Re: A basic Poisson AR(1) model not fitting to its parameter setup",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">On Sunday, August 23, 2015 at 2:34:30 PM UTC-4, <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">andre.p...@googlemail.com</a> wrote:<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0;margin-left:0.8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">The pairs plot looks not so good, but I have no idea how<div>to fix the correlations between the parameters. </div></div></blockquote></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><br>High correlations between parameters, by themselves, are not a problem. The main problem you are looking for in a pairs plot is where divergent transitions or transitions that exceed max_treedepth occur, but there do not seem to be any in this case. <br><br>The multimodality (probably across chains) is a problem and is evident in the pairs plot. The solution is likely to do a variant of the Matt trick for AR1 processes. Instead of declaring a vector parameter nu and doing <br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"font-family:monospace\"><span style=\"color:#000000;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"color:#000\">segment</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\">segment</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">y_r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span></span></span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>in the model block. You should declare a vector parameter nu_z, a local vector nu, and do<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sqrt</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> square</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">phi</span><span style=\"color:#660\">))</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nu_z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> y</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nu_z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>nu_z </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>y </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> poisson_log</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><br>That way you have no prior dependence among the elements of nu_z and no prior dependence with mu and phi.<br><br>Ben<br><br><br><br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
