   "subject": "Re: A basic Poisson AR(1) model not fitting to its parameter setup",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Dear Ben, Andrew, STAN users,<div><br></div><div><div>the model works perfectly fine now. I attached the to whoever wants to use it.</div><div>Feel free, for any purpose.</div><div><br></div><div>The ARMA model on: </div><div>6.4 pg. 84 2.7.0 shows: </div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><div>nu[1] &lt;- mu + phi * mu;   <span style=\"font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif\">// assume err[0] == 0</span></div></div></code></div><br></div><div>Isn&#39;t with err[0] == 0 the model AR(1) and thus nu[1] should be </div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\">nu[1] &lt;- mu / (1-phi); </font></div></code></div><br>Here&#39;s a link to Expected Value of ARMA(1,1):</div><div><a href=\"http://mathhelpforum.com/advanced-statistics/178529-expected-value-variance-arma-1-1-model.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://mathhelpforum.com/advanced-statistics/178529-expected-value-variance-arma-1-1-model.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792463873572000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1Fw9mgpSOUhXzmm2Ng0WZn\">http://mathhelpforum.com/advanced-statistics/178529-expected-value-variance-arma-1-1-model.html</a><br></div><div><br></div><div> I changed Ben&#39;s suggestions slightly: <br></div><div><br></div><div><div style=\"border:1px solid rgb(187,187,187);word-wrap:break-word;background-color:rgb(250,250,250)\"><code><div><font color=\"#660066\"><span style=\"color:#000\">  </span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">2</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">T</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\">  nu</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> mu </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> phi </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><b><span style=\"color:#000\">nu</span></b><b><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">-</span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span></b><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sigma </span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> nu_z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">t</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span></font><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></div></code></div><div><br></div><div>Andre </div></div></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
